CAT = "cpatcpFormation"; SEG = "FRM"
Q = [
("A business owned by one person, with no state filing and unlimited personal liability for its debts, is:",
 "A sole proprietorship", ["A single-member LLC", "A general partnership", "An S corporation"],
 "A sole proprietorship needs no filing to exist, has one owner and gives no liability shield. A single-member LLC requires a state filing and limits liability."),
("Which business entity must have at least one owner with unlimited personal liability?",
 "A limited partnership", ["A limited liability company", "A limited liability partnership", "A C corporation"],
 "A limited partnership needs at least one general partner, who is personally liable. LLC members, LLP partners and shareholders have limited liability."),
("Which entity cannot have a nonresident alien as an owner?",
 "An S corporation", ["A C corporation", "A partnership", "An LLC taxed as a partnership"],
 "Section 1361(b)(1)(C) bars nonresident alien shareholders from S corporations. C corporations, partnerships and LLCs may have foreign owners."),
("An owner contributes property with a basis of $60,000, a value of $100,000 and an $80,000 recourse mortgage. In a corporation she will control 80% or more; in a 50/50 general partnership she would bear half the debt. Her recognized gain is:",
 "$20,000 corp; $0 partnership", ["$0 corp; $20,000 partnership", "$20,000 in either form", "$0 in either form"],
 "Section 357(c) taxes the $20,000 of debt over basis in the corporation. In the partnership she keeps a $40,000 share of the debt, so net relief is $40,000, less than her $60,000 basis."),
("Two founders start a business: A contributes appreciated land for 50%, and B contributes only services for 50%. A's gain on the land is recognized:",
 "Only if they form a corporation", ["Only if they form a partnership", "In either form", "In neither form"],
 "A corporation fails section 351 because B's services are not property, so A, with only 50%, lacks 80% control. Section 721 gives nonrecognition in a partnership with no control test."),
("A C corporation holds assets with a basis of $200,000 and a value of $500,000 and no liabilities. It liquidates, pays 21% tax on its gain and distributes the rest to its sole shareholder, whose stock basis is $100,000. The shareholder's gain is:",
 "$337,000", ["$400,000", "$300,000", "$237,000"],
 "The corporation pays 21% of $300,000, or $63,000, and distributes $437,000. The shareholder's gain is $437,000 - $100,000 = $337,000, a second tax on the same appreciation.", 337000),
("An S corporation that was never a C corporation holds assets with a basis of $200,000 and a value of $500,000. Its sole shareholder's stock basis is $100,000. If it liquidates, the total gain taxed to the shareholder, including the pass-through, is:",
 "$400,000", ["$337,000", "$300,000", "$100,000"],
 "The $300,000 corporate gain passes through once and raises basis to $400,000, and the $500,000 received gives $100,000 more: $400,000 in all, at the shareholder level only.", 400000),
("When a partnership liquidates and distributes only property, not cash, to its partners, the partners usually:",
 "Recognize no gain", ["Recognize gain at fair value", "Pay a 21% entity-level tax", "Take a fair value basis"],
 "Section 731 taxes a partner only when cash exceeds outside basis. Distributed property takes a basis carried over from outside basis, deferring the gain."),
("A C corporation that converts to an LLC taxed as a partnership is treated for federal tax as:",
 "A taxable liquidation", ["A tax-free change of form", "An S election", "A section 351 transfer"],
 "Under the entity classification regulations, the change is a deemed distribution of assets in liquidation, taxed under sections 336 and 331."),
("Founders expect large early losses funded mostly by bank loans to the business, and want the entity's debt to give them basis for deducting the losses. Which form does this?",
 "A partnership", ["An S corporation", "A C corporation", "A personal service corporation"],
 "Partners include their share of partnership liabilities in outside basis (section 752). S shareholders get basis only for loans they make themselves."),
("A business that will reinvest all of its profits for many years may choose to be a C corporation mainly because:",
 "Kept profits face only 21% tax", ["Its losses pass to the owners", "Its dividends are deductible", "Its liquidation is tax-free"],
 "Retained earnings are taxed once at the flat 21% rate, which can be below owners' individual rates. The second tax comes only when profits are paid out or the company is sold."),
("A partnership agreement gives one partner 90% of depreciation deductions while profits are split equally. Which form allows this kind of special allocation?",
 "An LLC taxed as a partnership", ["An S corporation", "A C corporation", "A sole proprietorship"],
 "Partnerships may make special allocations with substantial economic effect (section 704(b)). S corporations must allocate items per share, per day."),
]
